From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent 9997: fetch AP stats from the object the name says, not a stale id

agent_fetch_ap_stats() works out which ubus object serves the statistics for a
BSS - wifi.ap.<ifname> for a plain BSS, wifi.apmld.<mld_ifname> for a link
affiliated to an MLD - writes it into objname, and then calls ap->obj_id
instead. The computed name is never used. Same defect class as 9994.

ap->obj_id is resolved once, in netif_alloc_ap(), from wifi.ap.<ifname>. On an
MLD node that id does not survive: the per-link BSSes are published at startup
and are gone once the links fold into the MLD entity, so the id goes stale
while the entry stays on re->aplist. Measured on 4g, 2026-08-03:

	ubus list | grep ^wifi\.ap
	    wifi.ap.ap-mld-1        wifi.apmld.ap-mld-1
	    wifi.ap.ap-mld-2        wifi.apmld.ap-mld-2

	iw dev | grep Interface
	    ap-mld-1   ap-mld-2   ap-mld-2.sta1

There is no wifi.ap.wlan0 / wlan1 / wlan2 object and no such netdev, yet the
agent carries wlan0, wlan1 and wlan2 on its aplist and asks their stale ids
every second:

	/tmp/mapc-log/mapagent.log
	    1230 x  Failed to get 'stats' (ret = 4)        [UBUS_STATUS_NOT_FOUND]
	       0 x  netif_alloc_ap: object:... missing over UBUS

Zero warnings from netif_alloc_ap is the evidence that the object existed when
the id was taken - this is staleness, not a lookup that never succeeded. The
apmld refresh path already carries the same reasoning in a comment (agent.c,
apmld_refresh(): "obj_id may be stale if wifimngr published the object after
the MLD entity was created during onboarding") and resolves by name; this makes
the stats path do the same.

The object answers once it is addressed by the right name:

	ubus call wifi.apmld.ap-mld-1 stats '{"band":5}'   -> stats object returned

Resolved lazily and cached in its own field rather than re-looked-up per tick:
the function runs once a second over every BSS, and ap->obj_id cannot be reused
as the cache because for an affiliated link the name resolves to the MLD
object, which the neighbour and status callers must not be handed. A failed
call drops the cache so a republished object is picked up on the next tick.

This repairs BSS statistics reporting (tx/rx bytes, packets per BSS). It does
not touch the station list - that path is bss_issue_stations(), which already
resolves apmld by name.

Not fixed here, and still open: num_stations from the HAL reads 0 while the
station list under the same BSS is correct.

--- a/src/agent.h
+++ b/src/agent.h
@@ -291,6 +291,11 @@
 	int bandwidth;
 	uint8_t bssid[6];
 	uint32_t obj_id;
+	/** object serving "stats" for this BSS - the MLD object when the BSS is
+	 *  an affiliated link, so it cannot share obj_id. WIFI_OBJECT_INVALID
+	 *  (0, as calloc leaves it) means "resolve on next use".
+	 */
+	uint32_t stats_obj_id;
 	char ssid[33];
 	char standard[32];
 	char radio_name[16];
--- a/src/agent.c
+++ b/src/agent.c
@@ -4841,7 +4841,22 @@ static void agent_fetch_ap_stats(struct agent *a)
 			if (band)
 				blobmsg_add_u32(&bb, "band", band);
 #endif
-			ubus_call_object_args(a->ubus_ctx, ap->obj_id, &bb, "stats", parse_ap_stats, bss);
+			/* Resolve objname, not ap->obj_id: the id taken in
+			 * netif_alloc_ap() is stale once the per-link BSS is
+			 * folded into the MLD entity, and for an affiliated
+			 * link objname names the MLD object anyway.
+			 */
+			if (ap->stats_obj_id == WIFI_OBJECT_INVALID)
+				ap->stats_obj_id = ubus_get_object(a->ubus_ctx,
+								   objname);
+
+			if (ap->stats_obj_id != WIFI_OBJECT_INVALID &&
+			    ubus_call_object_args(a->ubus_ctx,
+						  ap->stats_obj_id, &bb,
+						  "stats", parse_ap_stats, bss))
+				/* republished under a new id - resolve again */
+				ap->stats_obj_id = WIFI_OBJECT_INVALID;
+
 			blob_buf_free(&bb);
 		}
 	}
